course: dictionary
lesson: 9795
title: Building suspense
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Suspense is the excited or worried feeling of waiting to find out what will happen next. Writers build it on purpose so that you keep turning the page. «Scary music is used to build suspense: it tells you something might happen before anything does.» The music does not hurt you, but it warns you that danger may be coming.
explain: Writers have tools for building suspense. Short sentences speed things up. Repetition, as in «closer… closer… closer», slows time down as the danger comes nearer. A question in the reader's mind makes you wait for the answer.
explain: Suspense can also be spoiled. «Revealing the danger too fast removes the suspense.» If the monster is shown on page one, there is nothing left to wait for. Hold the answer back a little longer, and the reader keeps reading.
fact: Suspense is the excited or worried feeling of waiting to find out what happens next.
fact: Writers build suspense on purpose, so that you want to keep reading.
fact: Repetition can build suspense and make time feel slow.
fact: An ellipsis is exactly three dots; it can show suspense, a trailing-off voice, or missing words.
fact: To keep someone in suspense means to make them wait for news.
exercise: «Scary music is used to build suspense: it tells you something might happen before anything does.» What is suspense in this sentence? | A happy song played at a party | A loud noise that scares the animals | A word for a long, boring story | *A worried feeling of waiting for what happens next
exercise: «Repeating closer… closer… closer slows time down and builds suspense.» Which tool builds suspense in this sentence? | *Repetition | Using a map of the town | Writing a long title | Choosing a happy ending
exercise: Which of these is a phrase meaning to keep someone in suspense? | To tell them the ending at once | To make them laugh out loud | *To make them wait for news | To make them fall asleep
test: Why does «Revealing the danger too fast removes the suspense» make sense? | *Because there is nothing left to wait for once the danger is shown | Because readers like to be told everything at once | Because suspense means the story has ended | Because danger is always boring
test: Mia writes «She opened the door…» with three dots. Why might she do this? | To tell the reader the answer straightaway | To show she has spelled a word wrong | To mark the start of a new chapter | *To show a trailing-off voice or missing words, which can build suspense
exam: Which sentence builds suspense best? | Tea was ready at six. | *The door creaked open… and something breathed in the dark. | We went to the shop for bread. | The dog barked.
